add('shared-benchmarks','Three Models. The Same Benchmark Suite.',75,'S1MB / ENGLISH V1 · SNAPSHOT 21 OCT 2026',`<p class="benchmark-lead">137 benchmarks · 26,269 judgments per model</p><p class="benchmark-scale">Baseline-adjusted scores, 0–100 · higher is better</p><table class="benchmark-table"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Noul</th><th>Choice</th><th>Score</th><th>Task avg.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>TypeSafe Jev <span>1.13</span></th><td>64.63</td><td>67.22</td><td>46.92</td><td>59.59</td></tr><tr><th>GPT-6 Luna <span>Decisions API</span></th><td>54.54</td><td>56.23</td><td>36.85</td><td>49.21</td></tr><tr><th>AutoTrust <span>JEV-27B</span></th><td>67.65</td><td>67.73</td><td>54.98</td><td>63.45</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="benchmark-detail">Task average gives equal weight to Noul, Choice and Score.<br>These are normalized task scores, not accuracy percentages.</p><p class="benchmark-protocol">Published S1MB runs · complete coverage · Luna’s 17 English refusals count as errors.</p>`,"This table compares three decision interfaces on the S1MB English suite. It covers 137 benchmarks and 26,269 judgments per model. Read the rows in order: TypeSafe, GPT-6 Luna Decisions, then AutoTrust.\n\nThese are baseline-adjusted scores, not accuracy percentages. The task average gives equal weight to Noul, Choice and Score. AutoTrust leads this aggregate snapshot; that does not mean it wins every task.\n\nThe table measures task performance, not overall calibration, latency or cost. GPT-6 Luna here is the native Decisions API, not a chat model generating JSON or a frontier reasoning run.",['s1mb','s1mbScoring']);

add('shared-choice-benchmarks','A Closer Look at Shared Choice Tasks',75,'S1MB / ENGLISH V1 · SAME DATASETS AND SCORING',`<p class="benchmark-lead">Accuracy on four datasets · higher is better</p><table class="benchmark-table benchmark-choice-table"><thead><tr><th>Benchmark</th><th>TypeSafe<br><span>Jev 1.13</span></th><th>GPT-6 Luna<br><span>Decisions API</span></th><th>AutoTrust<br><span>JEV-27B</span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>BANKING77 <span>Banking intent · 100 decisions</span></th><td>77.0%</td><td>69.0%</td><td>73.0%</td></tr><tr><th>MASSIVE <span>100 cases · 200 decisions</span></th><td>88.5%</td><td>84.0%</td><td>89.0%</td></tr><tr><th>CLINC <span>Intent · 151 decisions</span></th><td>91.4%</td><td>82.1%</td><td>88.7%</td></tr><tr><th>GSM8K <span>Four-choice math · 100 decisions</span></th><td>85.0%</td><td>69.0%</td><td>81.0%</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="benchmark-protocol">Same recorded inputs across all three models. Fixed subsets of the full suite.<br>Runs: TypeSafe 28 Sep · GPT 14 Oct · AutoTrust 10 Oct 2026.</p>`,"These four examples make the aggregate easier to interpret. The first three involve intent classification; GSM8K is adapted into a four-choice math task. Here the figures are ordinary accuracy on the listed subsets.\n\nThe recorded inputs match across the three models. Notice that the ordering changes by task: AutoTrust leads MASSIVE, while TypeSafe leads the other three shown. Small differences on small subsets should not drive a universal ranking.\n\nThe engineering lesson is to compare on your own decision types, then look at the errors. An overall benchmark average cannot tell you which model is best for a particular workflow.",['s1mb']);

sources.medicalComparison = ['Madrid-García & Merino-Barbancho · Tables 2 & S7', 'https://arxiv.org/html/2609.34024v4'];
add('reasoning-benchmark','What Does Reasoning Buy?',105,'SAME QUESTIONS / JEV & GPT-6 SOL · 26 SEPTEMBER 2026',`<p class="benchmark-scale">Two medical Choice benchmarks · accuracy and recorded serving cost</p><table class="benchmark-table reasoning-table"><thead><tr><th>Task / model</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>p50</th><th>p95</th><th>USD / 1K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="task-heading"><th colspan="5">PubMedQA · 500 research abstracts</th></tr><tr><th>Jev 1.13</th><td>78.4%</td><td>0.27 s</td><td>0.35 s</td><td>$0.031</td></tr><tr><th>GPT-6 Sol · none</th><td>76.6%</td><td>1.19 s</td><td>1.62 s</td><td>$1.34</td></tr><tr><th>GPT-6 Sol · medium</th><td>78.2%</td><td>1.68 s</td><td>3.84 s</td><td>$1.85</td></tr><tr class="task-heading"><th colspan="5">DiagnosisArena-MCQ · 915 diagnostic cases</th></tr><tr><th>Jev 1.13</th><td>59.8%</td><td>0.28 s</td><td>0.37 s</td><td>$0.032</td></tr><tr><th>GPT-6 Sol · none</th><td>79.2%</td><td>1.32 s</td><td>1.82 s</td><td>$1.46</td></tr><tr><th>GPT-6 Sol · medium</th><td>82.4%</td><td>2.39 s</td><td>5.14 s</td><td>$2.75</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="reasoning-lesson">On complex cases, even Sol without reasoning has a large lead.</p><p class="reasoning-method">None / medium = reasoning effort. Jev: OpenRouter, sequential. Sol: OpenAI, 4 concurrent.<br>Client-side timings; Jev billed cost, Sol estimated from tokens. One run per condition.</p>`,"This is a separate study using the same questions for Jev and GPT-6 Sol. None and medium refer to Sol’s reasoning effort. The columns show accuracy, median and tail latency, and cost per thousand items.\n\nOn PubMedQA, Jev and medium-reasoning Sol have similar accuracy. On difficult diagnostic cases, Sol has a large advantage even without reasoning. So the difference cannot be explained by reasoning tokens alone.\n\nThe recorded costs and timings are useful context, but serving conditions differ: Jev used OpenRouter sequentially; Sol used the OpenAI API with four concurrent requests. Do not treat the timing ratio as a controlled model-speed comparison.",['medicalComparison']);

